{%- if is_ml_shaped %}
### One notebook per stage

Nine notebooks, one per stage of `{{ ml_source_root }}/`, in the order the stages
run. This is deliberately **not** one pipeline notebook: each one answers a single
question and can be run, re-run, and argued with on its own. A single
`run.py`-shaped notebook is what a function library without stage boundaries
produces, and it is unreviewable — you cannot tell which step made a number move.

| Notebook | Imports | Answers |
|---|---|---|
| `00_data_contract.py` | `ml.ingest` | Does the frame match its declared contract? |
| `01_eda.py` | `ml.transform` | Shape, dtypes, missingness pattern, target balance |
| `02_bivariate.py` | `ml.transform` | Which features carry signal — and which suspiciously well? |
| `03_features.py` | `ml.feature_engineering` | Which derived features earn their place? |
| `04_selection.py` | `ml.selection` | Which candidates exist, and which will actually run? |
| `05_training.py` | `ml.workflows` | Out-of-fold scores vs the baseline |
| `06_evaluation.py` | `ml.evaluation` | Metric *spread*, not just the mean |

The chain stops at evaluation. The two stages after it ship as module-and-test
only, with no notebook yet: choosing an operating point is
`ml/evaluation/threshold.py` (`choose_threshold`, which reports whether the point
was picked in-sample), and post-deployment drift is `ml/monitoring/`.

**Logic lives in the module; the notebook holds narrative, calls, and plots.** If
you write a validation rule or a metric in a notebook, it belongs in a stage
module with a test — `sys.path` is set to `{{ py_project_root }}/` at the top of
each one so the import is available.

Each notebook reads `data/raw/dataset.csv` and falls back to a synthetic frame
(`_sample_data.py`) when that file is absent, so all seven run on a fresh clone.
The synthetic frame is deliberately awkward — imbalanced target, a planted leak,
non-uniform missingness, a high-cardinality identifier, and a group column with
repeats — because a clean frame lets every stage pass without demonstrating what
the stage is for.

`01_eda.py` is bivariate-first: every section compares a feature *against the
target* rather than cataloguing columns in isolation. Two of its cells are
load-bearing and easy to skip. The **leakage audit** runs before any correlation
work, because a leaked feature does not raise an error — it returns a great score
and reads as success. The **handoff cell** at the bottom is its actual output: the
`CATEGORICAL` / `NUMERIC` / `ORDINAL` / `EXCLUDED` lists that feed
`{{ ml_source_root }}/selection/`. It reconciles its own counts, so a column
nobody decided about is reported rather than silently dropped.
